# Battle Rules

## 최신 공통 판정·보상 기반 (2026-10-26)

- 아래 과거 assisted 최소 .24/.40초·유예 .06초·Counterable의 X/C 설명은 폐기한다. 성공창은 기본 Z .12/X .22/C .18초, 유예 .03초이며 표시 CueWindow는 기존 길이를 보존한다. 이동/애니는 멈추지 않는다. 첫 예고 대응(조기/틀린 키/동시 입력/재준비 중 포함)은 확정하고 정답 키 재탐색을 허용하지 않는다. 배우 소유 공통 재준비 .18초는 짧은 공격 길이 절반 이하로 제한한다.
- 홀드 Z는 타격 뒤에도 일반65%감소로 유지한다. 대응 중 비모달 기록의 확인 입력 소비는 양보하지만 메뉴 확인 홀드의 방어 누출 차단은 유지한다. Counterable6과CounterOnly7 모두C전용이며 enum 숫자/실제 UI·저작 안내를 일치시킨다.
- 기본 AP는 턴2/저스트2/적 행동당 아군별 합계6이다. 모든 적 실행 root는 BeginParryRewardAction 수명을 공유하며 중첩 호출/중단/옛Dispose로 보상 예산을 새로 열지 않는다. 장비의 명시 보상·고유 자원은 별도이며 C 중복 저스트 AP는 없다. Lab만 최종최대40/시작20, 본편 성장·저장/전투 후 AP 유지 규칙은 보존한다.
- 공격 QTE 자동 옵션은 기본false, 기존 입력 창의 시계를 유지하고 정상 성공 피해만 준다. 방어에는 적용하지 않는다. 마지막 결과에도 pause/cancel/owner 가부를 재검사한다. 수동 prompt는 배우 배제를 고려한 일관된 측면 위치이며 스킬 키/타수/횟수 데이터는 변경하지 않는다.
- 누적 상태10/25/50 단계는 화상/감전/출혈/중독의 새 상태 피해 기여분만1.25/1.5/2배로 강화한다. 프로필에서 조정/해제 가능, 냉기/저항/자연 증감/소수 이월은 유지한다. 단계 상승만 기존 아이콘으로 강조하고 피해 글자/HUD폭을 늘리지 않는다.
- ItemData 선택 payload는 전투 누적 부여/소비 피해다. 무효/취소에 소모하지 않고 정수 누적0이라도 소수 잔량 변화는 유효 효과다. 성공한 아이템 행동당 ItemUsed5를1회 적용하고 광역 대상 수만큼 반복하지 않는다. 소비 피해의 기존 상태 이벤트와 일반 피해 기록을 중복 가산하지 않는다.
- 보스 HP presenter는 한 대상의 HP/비율/최대8개 표시 마커만 관찰한다. HP변경 없는 적 이탈도 next/hide하고 종료/재Bind/Disable/Destroy에 자기 구독/트윈을 정리한다. 페이즈/보상/무적/저장과 적이름분기는 없다. 평타·스킬구성·전조말풍선·보스별재설계는 별도 기획이다.

설정/검증: AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-10-26-combat-feedback-foundation.md.

## 반복 스킬 잠금과 C 성공 포커스 (2026-10-26)

- 현재 로컬 DOTween은 자식 Tween·실행 항목·콜백 없는 `Sequence().AppendInterval(...)`의 Startup을 실패로 처리한다. C의 단순 소유 대기는 실제 float Tween을 쓰고 Target/pause/취소/SetAutoKill(false)를 유지한다. 관리형 대체도 이 시작 실패를 포함해야 하며, 실제 DLL Startup 검사와 서비스 전체 회귀를 함께 사용한다.

- 임시 잠금은 PlayerCharacter의효과소유자별집합이다. Skills/SkillData/저장목록을삭제하거나수정하지않는다. BattleSkillLockScope는선택적Entry설정과기존flag를관찰하고본인정상행동시작/완료2회마다최대3개를다시추첨한다. 발동중인행동/C반격/취소는세지않고이전잠금을누적하지않는다. 종료/이탈/Disable는자기소유상태를정리한다.
- 표시CanUse와메뉴/대상확정/AP직전실행가부를동일하게유지한다. 잠긴행은탐색가능/회색,확정은빨강흔들림·비모달설명이며턴/AP를쓰지않는다. 포인터도기존대상선택/소비게이트를통과한다. 다른메인탭클릭으로이전목록entry를확정하지않는다.
- C성공보상연출만큰후퇴/명시정수2배focus/1초/접근공격/적뒤돌파/pairedreturn을쓴다. CameraController소유scope로최종출력까지보정하고일반Lenslock/PP/HUD/적공격시계를변경하지않는다. 죽은적에이동모션을재생하지않고명중후돌파와취소정리는유지한다.

## 현재 기본 정책 — 모든 적 공격 비차단 (2026-10-26)

- 아래 과거 opt-in/Ready·전조 대기/가드 접점 정지/국소 감속 설명은 이 정책이 대체한다. 실제 적 공격은 접근·비행·모션 중 전조와 대응 창을 진행한다. 일반 명중 TimeWindow와 기존 피해/가드65%/AP/입력 유예/재준비를 보존하며 전역시간은 바꾸지 않는다.
- 모든 일반 단일 근접은 연속 scope를 사용하고 가드에도 관통한다. 일반은 원래 명중 시간 전체에 접근하고 난무 복사본만 이동 기준 접점을 쓴다. ContinuousMeleeMotion 런타임 플래그를 일반 공격에 임의로 true로 설정하면 반응 시간이 난무 시간으로 줄어드므로 금지한다.
- C/광역은 중앙 배치를 유지하며 창 시작부터 클립 명중 프레임을 실제 충돌에 맞춘다. VFX는 원래 impact lead에서1회다. 투사체는 물리 도착에 시각 반환하되 늦은 판정과 피해는1회만 확정한다.
- 적 전투만 Wait/Ready/Windup/중복 지상 TargetFront·AttackStaging을 생략하고 실루엣은 병행한다. 이야기/아군 대기·실제 특수 이동·메뉴 pause·C 성공 반격/복귀·예약 턴은 유지한다. 동기화 탐색도 무시되는 준비만 건너뛰며 실제 특수 이동은 생략하지 않는다.
- 모든 실행 root는 최종 반응 drain 전에 Defer=false로 복구한다. 진행 중 피격/회피를 중간 Reset으로 끊지 않는다. 구형 Scene/Prefab/SkillData를 대량 재저장하거나 Source/Save ID를 변경하지 않는다. 현재 제작·검증은 AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-10-26-global-enemy-attack-flow.md 참고.

## 적 후속 패턴·연속 돌파·C전용 (2026-10-26)

- phase는기존Scenarioflag가소유한다. 선택적EnemyData.FollowupPatterns는flag/필요정상행동수/Skill/Once 데이터이며코드에적이름·HP·스킬ID분기를넣지않는다. 배우별BattleEnemyActionPatternState는행동시작flag를캡처하고정상완료에만횟수/once를확정한다. 기존순환index와예약강스킬정책을보존하고취소/예고/Wait를완료로세지않는다.
- SkillData.RecoveryTurns 기본0,현재적TurnQte정상실행완료/C반격완료에만예약한다. 다음자기차례1회를소비하고CC저항/기절/저장을사용하지않는다.모듈중단은대기선택만취소,전투Dispose에서상태를모두정리한다.
- Action_EnemyAssaultCombo는EnemyOnly/단일대상이고각단계타수/처음끝판정·이동·준비·공중·무작위확률을데이터화한다. 매타기존DefenseWindow와Damage만1회실행하고피해를별도로더하지않는다.공중Entry와기존방어clock의지면Contact를연결한다. 새MeleeApproachHeight/PassThroughOnGuard는기존default0/false를유지한다.
- ContinuousMotion은가드관통과함께사용하는선택모드(defaultfalse/Labtrue)다. 매타준비/고정판정대기/감속을반복하지않고실제접점으로판정한다. 유예중에도관통하고결과시접점으로되감지않는다. 반응대기지연보다이동ScopeFinish를먼저수행하며전체끝에서반응을정리한다. 중간ResetDefenseReactionLock/현재회피위치추적/전역시간변경은금지한다. 최종C와기존개별공격에는적용하지않는다.
- DefenseRequirement.Counterable6/CounterOnly7 모두C전용이다. 모든cue/result/Matches는IsCounterRequirement와MatchesActive를공유한다. C의cue를없는X창0과Min하지않는다. 최종VFX는DefenseWindow공격시작에서실행해야C성공의남은Timeline중단에도빠지지않는다.
- BattleSkillVfxLifetime lease는세대토큰으로AutoDespawn/재사용/옛취소를분리한다. normaltail은hostactive/scene/lifetime아래허용하며취소는자기효과만반환한다. 풀PS의stopActionDestroy는자식도삭제해재사용을깨므로None+AutoDespawn수명을쓴다.

## 고정3줄 기록·페이즈 표시 후속 (2026-10-26)

- Lab사슬배치는사용자빨간선정정으로ScreenCorners(전체640×480)/Inset64/CanvasSortingOrder-50다. 전투영역안쪽이아니라화면네모서리의인접한두변을가로지르며각도BL+45/TL-45/TR+45/BR-45다. HUD(-100)위·대화32766아래로표시하고raycast는하지않는다. 다른프로필의PlayfieldCorners기본/-200은유지한다.

- 상단 기록 Backg는 스프라이트 없는 Simple 검정 alpha0.5 사각형이다. SettingPanel9slice나말풍선꼬리를재사용하지않는다. 가로정렬은TMP프리팹의Center설정을보존하고코드로TopLeft를강제하지않는다. 스크롤세로기준Top은유지한다. 다른대화창/원본그림에는확장적용하지않는다.

- 아래10/25의 즉시전체표시 정책은 폐기됐다. CombatLogPresenter는 한 행동의 모든 결과를 한 메시지로 전달하고 즉시 표시/유지 시간을 강제하지 않는다. BattleNarrationUI의 공용 프리팹 데이터가 고정 크기/폰트/줄수/타이핑·유지·스크롤을 소유한다. 기본480×72/14px/3줄/0.015초·0.5초·0.1초다.
- TMP 전체 rich text를 유지하고 최초 lineInfo 문자 범위/기준선을 캐시한다. firstVisibleCharacter는0을 유지한다. 이 값을 올리면 TMP가 앞 문자를 레이아웃에서 제외하므로 전체 본문 위치 스크롤과 혼용하지 않는다. maxVisibleCharacters와 기존 RectMask2D로 줄 출력/위쪽 스크롤을 제어한다. 글 분량으로 창/폰트를 확대하지 않는다.
- Z 현재 줄 완성/다음 새 입력으로 다음 줄/마지막 줄 종료를 분리하고 기존 GameInput 소비를 유지한다. 모달/일시정지는 세 시계를 함께 멈추고 큐 종료/Clear/Disable/본문 파괴는 Busy/위치/가시 범위를 정리한다. 이야기/짧은말풍선의 TextAnimator는 별개다.
- 사슬 Profile의 SpriteScale은 원본 반복 높이에도 적용한다. 코너별 Mirror와 회전은 공유재질을 유지하며 그림/이동방향을 같이 대칭화한다. 음량0~1/기본0.18, Lab0.45이며 BGM/shared source를 바꾸지 않는다.
- Lab 제브 일반4종7창은 ScreenWide, C는 중앙이다. 이미 화면 끝 접근이 있는 스킬에 중복 AttackStaging Move를 넣지 않는다. 공통 피격 대상 전진과 가드/저스트 접점 정지는 유지한다.

## 중앙 상단 행동 결과 기록 (2026-10-25)

- 피격/저체력의 자동 DamageTaken/LowHp 호출은 하지 않는다. 이야기 대화와 별도 턴/스킬 말풍선은 삭제하지 않는다.
- `BattleCombatLogPresenter`만 실제 OnHPChanged/OnAPChanged 전후 차이와 상태 종류 차이를 수집한다. OnDamageDealt/OnElementalDamage를 다시 더하거나 피해·회복 함수를 호출하지 않는다. 새 bind는 기준값이며 수치/기간 갱신을 새 부여로 표시하지 않는다.
- 결과는 기존 피해 배치가 끝난 뒤 대상별로 합산한다. C 반격 하위 Ended가 Z방어 도중 새 결과창을 열지 않게 현재 바깥 행동을 유지한다. 아이템·턴 시작 효과도 결과 안내 대기 전에 배치를 닫는다. 일반 결과는 기존 중앙창의 안전 경계에서 즉시전체표시/자동 종료/Z즉시넘김이며 실시간 비차단 토스트가 아니다.
- 전투 종료/abort/모듈 종료에서는 배치 Clear·상태 초기화보다 관찰기 해제가 먼저다. 중단된 모듈은 HUD Disable→Enable로 수집을 재개하지 않는다. 판정·기절/빙결·저항·턴 알고리즘은 바꾸지 않는다.
- 새 문구는 `battle.log.*` 번역 키와 실제 표시명 getter를 사용한다. 기존 내레이션은 기본false인 RevealImmediately 옵션으로 타이핑 정책을 보존한다.

## 이야기 후퇴와 임시 참가자 (2026-10-24)

- `IEncounterStoryWithdrawalPolicy` opt-in 조우만 `StoryWithdrawn=4`를 사용한다. 요청은 source 객체를 확인해 예약하고 행동/결과 안전 경계에서 처리한다. 조기 처치 변환도 명시적 opt-in이며 일반 승리/패배 규칙은 유지한다. 보상·처치 기록·도주 후 접촉 억제·GameOver로 위장하지 않는다.
- `IEncounterRosterPolicy.BattleRosterCharacterIds`는 CharacterData ID이다. CharacterSaveData.CharacterID는 표시 이름이므로 비교 키로 사용하지 않는다. 생성·후열·저장 backwrite까지 동일 필터를 적용하고 실제 저장 파티는 재정렬/삭제하지 않는다.
- `OnPlayerActionCompleted`는 실제 공격/스킬/아이템 효과가 완료된 행동만 전달한다. 취소·무효·미소비 아이템·이전 세대 콜백을 학습 성공으로 처리하지 않는다. 방어 결과는 기존 QTEManager의 실제 DefenseQteResult를 관찰하고 새로운 타이밍 판정을 만들지 않는다.

## Battle viewport/HUD clearance (2026-10-23)

- Strict pixel output supersedes the earlier adaptive Lens4 design. PPU32/ref640x480 permits Lens7.5/n (integer n>=1), not arbitrary4. Battle defaults to art-scale1/Lens7.5, minimum request3.75. Choose an outward-snapped safe lens once per session, then FitAtFixedSize adjusts center only. Never zoom on movement/hop/return/hits; oversized authored layouts warn once rather than silently violating the pixel lattice. Recalculate only for a new session or changed pixel profile/mode.
- `BattleUIController.TryGetBattlePlayfieldScreenRect` reserves bottom162px/top48px plus8px margins. Cache body bounds once per actor (retry the cached Renderer until its first Sprite exists). Include front line/enemies; skip perspective/tilted3D/Timeline. Never move actors/HUD to compensate. No extra camera/RT/GPU pass or per-frame GetComponent.
- `CinemachineContinuousPixelZoom` retains its serialized name but never toggles native CinemachinePixelPerfect. Use returned correction in Body AND Finalize after hold/safety; snap final XY, preserve Z and suppress Dutch in strict mode. Brain blends after vcam Finalize, so correct the owned live output Lens again via CameraUpdatedEvent; Hold Lens takes precedence, and subscriptions detach on disable/destroy. URP PPC owns final render PixelSnap, not another matrix rewrite.
- Presentation Release and viewport policy owner have different lifetimes: Release clears hold only. Temporary HUD hiding/pause keeps both policy and fixed Lens, skipping center fitting only. Timeline/Controller disable/session end explicitly unbind policy; a reset callback cannot produce a one-frame policy gap. Session Lens survives temporary Timeline changes and clears on actual session end. Preserve tokens/cancellation/leases; stale cleanup cannot release a newer Timeline. Engine/device verification remains separate from managed tests. See AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-10-23-camera-pixel-stability.md.

## Optional enemy melee pass-through (2026-10-23)

- `Action_DefenseWindow.UseMeleePassThrough` is opt-in, currently only ZEV BlinkSlash/ComboSlash. Keep other skills unchanged. Exclude projectile, directly multi-target, Counterable, and telegraph-only windows; SequentialMelee may opt in separately for each actual target.
- SkillContext owns `BattleEnemyMeleePresentationScope`. Capture the defender position once, drive the approach DOTween from QTE authored time, hold contact through late grace, and consume the exit only AFTER existing damage/VFX via WaitForPendingDefenseReaction. Guard/JustGuard stop at contact. Never duplicate damage or chase dodging targets.
- Keep defense→VFX/damage or adjacent defense→SequentialMelee; do not insert other movement/waits before the impact consumer. Windup may already own Ready; opt-in still waits for camera preparation before opening defense without restarting Ready.
- Cancel the melee scope BEFORE restoring SequentialMelee actorStart or defender staging. Kill only owned Tweens; stale Dispose must not overwrite a new scope. Ordinary/scenario roots share this lifecycle. Camera token/defense hold/Timeline/C takeover/modal/scene cleanup remain authoritative; HUD and global time are unaffected.
- Tuning and validation boundary: `AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-10-23-enemy-melee-pass.md`.

## Lab background-only parry ripple (2026-10-19)

- Only confirmed Z `DefenseInput.Parry` requests `ParryBackgroundRipple` from the defender Center pivot. Do not hook raw input, Animator state, normal guard, dodge or C counter. No damage/reward/timing rule changes.
- Lab Renderer2D only: BeforePP exact-size Point mask, Background sorting-layer range white then all higher ranges black; warp only when BOTH destination and candidate source are permitted background. Never identify sorting layers with a GameObject LayerMask. Keep Overlay HUD and existing ScreenSplit separate.
- Frame sprites/Tilemaps only; GPU SpriteSkin keywords and custom material vertex deformation are not inherited by override materials. Do not claim those are supported. Idle adds no GPU pass/copy; active adds one mask and one warp. No permanent CameraSortingLayerTexture/extra camera/package edits. State is per camera/latest request, once-per-frame unscaled time, menu pause and owner/scene/feature cleanup. Keep the documented manual GPU validation boundary.

## 챕터 1 확정 전투 규칙 (2026-10-16)

- 일반 Z 방어는 피해 65% 감소(배율 0.35), 저스트가드는 피해 0 + AP, X 회피는 피해 0이며 기본 AP 보상은 없다. 명시적인 장비/스킬 효과는 별도다. Counterable과 회피 전용 제한, 후한 전조 입력 창과 늦은 입력 유예는 유지한다.
- `PlayerAttackQtePolicy`는 공격 QTE 성공을 정상 피해 1.0으로, 실패를 감소 피해(기본 0.5)로 계산한다. 공격 QTE만으로 추가 AP/고유 자원을 주지 않는다. 스킬에 작성한 압력 소비/강화/완료 효과와 방어 보상은 제거하지 않는다.
- 전투 후 HP/AP는 유지하고 전투 상태만 전체 참가자에서 제거한다. 보상 EXP는 `GlobalDataManager.GrantPartyExperience`에 한 번 전달한다. 개인별 레벨업 표시가 여러 개여도 EXP 보상은 하나다.
- `EnemyRank.Boss` 또는 도주 불가 필수 조우는 도주를 막고 진입 직전 메모리 재도전을 준비한다. 일반 도주 실패는 현재 행동을 소비하며 기존 속도 스케줄을 건너뛰지 않는다. 일반 패배는 최근 저장, 보스/필수전은 아이템 등까지 복원한 직전 재도전을 사용한다. 훈련의 명시적인 필드 복귀 정책은 유지한다.
- 보스 승리는 보상 확정 후 자동저장을 예약한다. `BattleRetryCheckpoint.CompleteEncounter`는 패배 시 복사본을 보존하고 승리/도주에는 해제한다. 저장은 전투 중이 아니라 정리·대사·결과창이 끝난 안정된 탐색에서 수행한다.
- 메뉴 일지/정보 열람은 전투 진행 시간을 멈추되 음악은 유지한다. 챕터 1 안내 대상은 화상/감전이며 기존 상태 시스템을 삭제하거나 새 보스 밸런스를 추측해 작성하지 않는다.
- 전투 메뉴는 Escape/패드 Start/모바일 일시정지로 기존 OverworldMenuUI를 연다. 명령 선택·아군 행동·적 행동 중에만 허용하고 대사/진입/결과/후열 선택/로딩에서는 차단한다. C 반격 입력을 메뉴로 재사용하지 않는다.
- 게임 판정에 관여하는 unscaled 대기는 `BattleDelay`와 블록 경계의 pause 게이트를 사용한다. 공격/방어 애니메이션 완료의 예비 제한시간도 정지 기간을 제외한다. `timeScale=0`만으로 WaitForSecondsRealtime이나 실시간 deadline이 정지한다고 가정하지 않는다. 음악/HUD 장식과 피해/입력 시계의 소유권은 분리한다.

## Player-facing skill descriptions

- Keep SkillData.Description to one or two short effect-focused sentences. Do not expose hit counts, rapid-hit implementation, Z/X/C, QTE timing, camera choreography or lab instructions. Preserve useful status keyword colors and essential resource conditions. AP/target/resource details already have dedicated UI fields; do not duplicate the full technical specification. Keep production notes in action block labels and authoring documentation. Generated sample descriptions follow the same rule; never change mechanics to simplify copy.

## Reserve wave presentation

- The full transition, including narration, must release its own generation lock in finally. OnDisable cancels owned presentation tweens as well as the coroutine. Revalidate after state/party-change callbacks; stale cleanup must not unlock a newer wave. If only a presentation tween is killed, finish promotion/entry cleanup and continue existing turn flow rather than leaving ActionExecute locked.
- Damage batch override IDs must be cleared before Ended callbacks. Popup pools reject incomplete views; replacing a destroyed total view preserves the accumulated amount. Reserve both horizontal and vertical space, including peak pulse scale.

- All active allies must be defeated before the prepared living reserve wave can be promoted. BattleReserveChoiceUI asks Continue/GiveUp before any fade or promotion. Continue is the default; Cancel only selects Continue. GiveUp enters GameOver even for a training encounter's return-on-defeat policy, without activating reserves or writing a save. Choice input and coroutines are generation-owned; GameInput blocks underlying battle commands/defense. BattleManager then fades defeated sprites (default0.5s), promotes and enters reserves from camera-left (default0.55s, BattleMove→BattleIdle) only after Continue. BattlePartyWavePresentation owns tweens/snapshots, not HP or party creation. Abort/end/disable closes the prompt and restores presentation. No manual swaps.

## Per-impact cue baseline (2026-10-07)

- One impact cue is shown at the shared active success window, not at the earlier dodge preparation window. QTEManager may stabilize the camera earlier but must not show a dim pre-signal that rejects fresh Z/C. BattleTelegraphCue preparation-only API remains compatibility-only; ordinary runtime calls Spawn(prepareOnly:false) at the common window.
- Preserve existing generous input minima and late grace. No universal rhythm-preview/beat-memory system. Attack-ready completes before the response clock; each target/impact retains its own input consumption. Action_DefenseWindow.UseTelegraph defaults false for newly created blocks; explicit old/additional preparation and next-turn previews remain compatible. The optional extra preview must be authored distinctly from the actionable impact cue.

## Enemy single-target selection

- `BattleEnemyTargetSelector<T>` owns weighted random targeting/history shared by all enemies. The Turn QTE service supplies live eligibility and at most25% missing-HP priority. Reduce recent-four/last-target weight; forbid three consecutive selections if another eligible target exists. Skip Unity-null/dead/disabled/inactive actors. Select once per single-target action, retain its target across hits. Never read player input.
- AoE, waiting, and reserved preparation do not consume history. AoE's first-alive representative is only for presentation. Do not reset history at actor-turn/module boundaries or overwrite explicit scenario targets. New battle service = empty history; candidates always come from the current frontline.
- No per-enemy setting, scene wiring, random allocation, or saved state is needed. See the existing controller service tests and `AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-09-28-skill-block-authoring.md` for authoring rules.

## Seamless entry presentation

The shared prefab's BattleMenu and BattleSubMenu are siblings. Include BattleMenuUI.SubMenuRoot in the lower-HUD entry motion so the list, description and frame use the same alpha/translation. Do not rely on main-menu ancestry or reparent the authored HUD; retain ancestor/duplicate filtering in BattleHudEntryMotion.

Entry defaults are 2 seconds with a 0.5-second lens-only zoom. Before cover, use PlayerController.HoldForBattleEntry and Cutscene state, never BattleIdle or early NPC hiding. IsCovered is set only after an opaque frame has been yielded and generation revalidated. BattleManager then calls IEncounterPreparationSource, enters Battle mode, and prepares participants/UI/camera. The minimum black hold overlaps preparation; Reveal waits only its remaining duration. Preserve dedicated-scene behavior and release the input hold through existing SetBattleMode/abort cleanup.

The seamless entry presentation is a separate visual boundary, not a turn/defense module: `SeamlessBattleHost` owns settings and a cached overlay presenter; BattleManager awaits cover, existing preparation, and reveal before starting existing scenario/module flow. Dedicated BattleScene must not invoke it. Honor per-encounter `IEncounterEntryPresentationPolicy` and `DialogueEncounterContext.PlayEntryPresentation`. Never recalculate turns or bypass dialogue/target selection for the entry animation. Camera changes go through CameraController tokens, with continuous-pixel-zoom restoration; the presenter only cancels its current token. Host abort/common seamless cleanup explicitly cancels the presenter before stopping coroutines. HUD entry snapshots independent lower RectTransforms (not BattleHudViewport's layout root), restores alpha/position/input on all exits, and must not double-tween nested parents/children. See `AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-10-02-battle-entry-reference.md`.

## Speed-based turn schedule

- `BattleSpeedTurnScheduler<T>` is the sole Turn QTE order/frequency calculator. Each actor starts with 1000 remaining work; arrival time is remaining / max(1, SPD). Advance discrete time to the earliest actor, then recharge only that actor. Simultaneous arrival is resolved by initial registration order, never concurrent execution.
- `BattleTurnQteModuleControllerService` synchronizes live frontline players/enemies at turn boundaries and status changes, including same-SPD duration refresh. Keep the active actor fixed and preserve remaining work on SPD changes and for surviving enemies during reserve promotion. New/revived participants begin fresh; inactive reserves do not accumulate readiness. Dispose/unsubscribe observers at module exit or service replacement/destruction.
- Actual action consumption and copied-state preview must share the algorithm. Queue size affects forecast length only. `BattleTurnQueueProjection` must not fill missing entries with an invented round. During an action, queue index 0 is the current actor and host CurrentActorIndex remains one past it for existing consumers.
- Turn calculation yields between actions/skips and rejects duplicate starts while an actor is active. Menus, animations, QTE, pause, and Game Module absence must not tick this schedule in wall-clock time.
- Keep the existing one-time fastest-player preemptive opening, actor-turn AP/status rules, and victory → reserve promotion → defeat priority. Speed increases therefore also increase AP recovery and per-actor status processing frequency. Forecast existing stat-effect expiry through read-only GetForecastSpeed/IsActiveAfterTurnBoundaries, including the active actor's pending end. Do not predict new status applications, damage/death or roster events.
- A copied forecast tracks each actor's future completed turns; its speed reader accounts for start/end expiry without calling live OnTick/OnRemove or changing HP/AP/flags. The old displayed suffix must survive ordinary completion and known expiry. Never assume temporary Haste persists forever or grant promised-but-invalid free turns to hide a wrong forecast. Reapplication at equal SPD can change future order, so suppress only unchanged actor sequences, not all same-SPD status events.
- Do not add serialized settings or rewrite battle assets for this schedule. Use resolved CharacterBase.SPD, including existing stat modifiers. See `AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-09-28-speed-turns.md`.
- Haste is authored through Action_ApplyStatus (HasteSpeedBonus defaults to 0.3). No stacking; refresh duration and retain stronger rate. Tick at target turn end and do not spend the already-active granting turn. Other status timing remains unchanged.
- Turn queue feedback belongs to BattleTurnQueueView/Icon: six reusable layout holders, reference + occurrence matching, unscaled visual-child move + synchronized pulse. Same visible order is a no-op for animation. Keep prefab mask clearance for the pulse; never tween the GridLayoutGroup-owned holders.

## Conditional resource boosts

- SkillResourceEffect never blocks an otherwise usable skill. BeginSkillResources checks the same definition and snapshots/consumes once. CompleteSkillResources grants once only while execution is valid. Cancelled casts do not refund committed cost or grant completion gain.
- Every hit uses SkillContext.EffectiveDamageMultiplier = QTE/current multiplier × resource multiplier. Resetting CurrentDamageMultiplier must not reset the cast's resource bonus.
- Both the ordinary turn runner and BattleSkillTimelineRunner call the same lifecycle. A final-target kill completes; cancellation/death/empty timelines do not grant rewards. Perfect-parry gain shares the existing once-per-defense-window reward path.

## Current Structure

- `BattleEncounterService`: common battle entry from overworld, dialogue, and triggers.
- `BattleManager`: current battle flow host. It handles setup, broad state changes, battle end, and return. `BattleTurnQteModuleControllerService` owns QTE turn/input/action flow through `IBattleTurnQteHost`; serialized settings remain in the host.
- `SeamlessBattleHost`: shared composition root for one BattleManager, PositionManager, and battle UI in both Room-based seamless Battle and the dedicated BattleScene. The prefab is the common source; the dedicated scene overrides only dedicated mode, UI activation, and its scene camera. It validates ownership, removes duplicate roots, and delegates emergency abort; it does not decide combat outcomes.
- `BattleUIController`: event-driven battle UI and targeting bridge.
- `QTEManager`: defense QTE and skill sequence QTE execution.
- `SkillData`: ScriptableObject skill data.
- `SkillActionBlock`: existing data-driven skill action timeline.
- `PositionManager`: battle slots, center position, and attack positions.

## Design Direction

- Do not expand `BattleManager` further when a narrower module or adapter can own the behavior.
- Keep `Assets/_Game/Content/Battle/Prefabs/System/SeamlessBattleHost.prefab` as the common source for dedicated and seamless battle runtime composition. Do not rebuild it from `BattleScene` or copy its runtime children into a scene.
- Keep `BattleEncounterService` as the common battle entry seam.
- Seamless victory, run, and Host abort must use BattleManager's shared cleanup boundary so player position/input, encounter context, temporary actors, UI, camera, and battle session state are restored together.
- Clear battle-scoped status effects for the full party roster (including reserve/defeated actors) and enemies before normal outro callbacks/save/rewards, and again idempotently on seamless abort or manager destruction. Do not clear them on a 3+3 wave change or Game Module switch.
- Ordinary enemy basic/AoE fallback strikes use `TakeDamage(..., DamageElement.Physical, enemy)` and publish the resolved damage. Keep `TakePureDamage` for explicitly defense-ignoring effects such as authored pure damage and status ticks.
- DOTween Sequences that can be canceled before their first update must use an owner ID and ID-based cleanup; killing only a nested child target does not own the parent Sequence.
- Treat `SkillActionBlock` as a legacy battle-skill timeline, not as the root of the future global Action Sequence system.
- Stun is evaluated before its start-of-turn duration tick. Completed attacks/skills/items notify action-bound effects once; skips/cancels do not. Bleed expires at turn end, after its last action damage, then victory/reserve/defeat checks run.
- Cancellable skill blocks own their Tween handles and pooled objects. Dispose must propagate from ActionDirector through adapter/runner to nested blocks; cleanup must not kill unrelated new Transform Tweens. Keep canceled QTE results from advancing to later damage blocks.
- Future flexible combat should run through Primary Mode / Game Module / Action Sequence concepts from `CONTEXT.md`.
- New battle modules should preserve `BattleSessionState`-like data such as HP, MP, status, enemies, party, and battle flags across module switches.
- `BattleSessionState` currently owns scenario identity, Primary Mode, opening/current Game Module continuity, and read-only `BattleParticipantSnapshot` entries for party/enemy HP, MP, alive state, and common status flags. Future HP/MP/status mutation ownership should extend battle-scoped state deliberately, not hide those facts inside an individual Game Module.
- Runtime actions and Game Modules should read battle-scoped state through `IBattleSessionStateReader` from `ActionExecutionContext`. Do not add new `BattleManager.Instance` reads just to learn scenario/module/participant state.
- Runtime actions and Game Modules should request HP/MP changes through `IBattleParticipantCommandRunner` from `ActionExecutionContext`. Do not add new direct `BattleManager.Instance` branches for shooter/boxing/minigame damage, healing, MP gain, or MP consumption.
- Scenario-authored HP/MP changes should use `battle.participant.damage`, `battle.participant.heal_hp`, `battle.participant.heal_mp`, or `battle.participant.consume_mp`; keep those actions routed through `IBattleParticipantCommandRunner`.
- Runtime actions and Game Modules should use `BattleSessionState` flags for temporary battle-scoped facts that need to survive Game Module switches. Scenario-authored flag writes should use `battle.flag.set` / `battle.flag.clear` through `IBattleSessionFlagStore`; do not hide shared phase facts in module-local booleans.
- Scenario-level battle flow should be authored through Battle Scenario Data / Encounter Definition and the `.agents/skills/hubtohome-scenario-authoring/` rules, not by expanding one-off branches inside `BattleManager`.
- Battle Event Rule subjects should use Scenario Subject IDs. Enemy rules resolve through `BattleScenarioSubjectResolver` and should primarily match `EnemyData.EnemyId`.
- New rules should use `BattleScenarioData.TriggerRules`, stable Scenario Event IDs, and Trigger Conditions. `BattleScenarioData.Rules` / `BattleEventRuleData` are legacy compatibility data mapped once by `BattleTriggerRuleCompatibilityMapper`; do not create a second runtime evaluator path or rewrite existing assets casually.
- Per-encounter battle scenarios can be passed through `BattleEncounterService.StartEncounter(..., battleScenarioData)` or assigned as the BattleManager default scenario. Dedicated battle scene transitions use `GlobalDataManager.PendingBattleScenario` as a runtime-only handoff.
- `OverworldEnemy._battleScenarioData` and `DialogueBattleNPC._fallbackBattleScenarioData` are the current serialized entry-point fields for per-encounter scenarios. Use those on cloned/new entries instead of changing existing ZEV wiring when comparing scenario architecture work.
- Battle event publication should go through `BattleScenarioRuntime`, not direct `BattleScenarioRuleRunner` / `BattleScenarioEventRouter` ownership inside `BattleManager`.
- Trigger timing belongs to the authored Trigger Rule. `BattleScenarioEventRouter` evaluates on publication, queues non-immediate matches, and releases them through enum timing or named checkpoint flushes. Do not delay raw event objects in new code.
- `PerEncounterMemory` battle rule state can be seeded into and exported from `BattleScenarioRuntime`, then stored in `GlobalDataManager` Encounter Memory outside battle. Do not serialize an in-progress battle session as save data.
- `BattleManager` should use `BattleEncounterMemoryRecorder` for encounter memory seed/start/result recording. Do not duplicate meet-count, defeated-state, or seen-beat recording branches directly inside battle flow.
- `BattleManager.OnBattleScenarioTriggersReady` is an emission seam for fired scenario triggers. Do not put sequence execution policy, module switching, or phase-specific branches directly in `BattleManager`; trigger sequences are queued and drained through `BattleScenarioExecutionGate`, then executed by `BattleScenarioActionBridge` and `ActionDirector`.
- `BattleScenarioActionBridge` is responsible for trigger-to-sequence lookup, child ActionExecutionHandle creation, sequential trigger execution, and failure propagation. Keep that logic out of `BattleManager`.
- `BattleScenarioActionBridge` must validate and bind `BattleScenarioTrigger.TargetInputsJson` against the target Sequence Input contract before playback.
- `BattleScenarioExecutionGate` owns ready-trigger queueing, deferred flush checkpoints, trigger emission, execution result reporting seam, and battle-flow blocking while scenario sequences run. Do not start scenario trigger coroutines directly from arbitrary BattleManager damage or state methods.
- Battle scenario action contexts are created through `BattleScenarioActionContextFactory`. Keep scenario field setup and `BattleScenarioData.Dialogues` registration there instead of adding dialogue setup branches to `BattleManager`.
- Battle scenario presentation commands use runner seams: `IAudioActionRunner` for `bgm.crossfade`, `IScreenTransitionRunner` for `screen.fade`, and `IGameModuleActionRunner` for `module.switch` / `module.start`. Do not hard-code BGM, fade UI, or module transition branches directly inside `BattleManager`.
- Battle camera presentation must go through `ICameraPresentationService` / `CameraController` and Cinemachine. Use `battle.camera.focus`, `battle.camera.reset`, and `battle.camera.shake` with `static`, `dynamic`, or `gameplay_safe` styles. Do not snap a CinemachineCamera Transform directly. Timeline playback owns an exclusive camera lease and must release it on completion, cancellation, and destruction before reset.
- Current battle scenario contexts inject `AudioManagerActionRunner` and `ScreenTransitionRunner`. `AudioManagerActionRunner` resolves BGM clip IDs through `BattleScenarioData.AudioClips` first and `ResourcesAudioClipResolver` fallback second, so scenario-specific BGM should be added to the scenario `audioClips` mapping instead of hard-coded in `BattleManager`.
- Game Module switching should go through `GameModuleRegistry` and `GameModuleActionRunner`. Concrete battle modules such as current QTE flow, shooter, or boxing should implement `IGameModuleRuntime` so Action Sequences can call stable module IDs without adding BattleManager branches.
- Battle must keep one `IGameModuleActionRunner` instance alive for the battle session. Do not recreate it for each Action Sequence; otherwise `CurrentModuleId` can reset to `OpeningModule` after a module switch.
- Register default battle modules through `BattleGameModuleRegistryFactory` or its successor, not by adding per-module registration branches directly into `BattleManager`.
- Current battle scenario contexts register `BattleTurnQteGameModuleRuntime` as `turn_qte` and the first non-QTE shell `BattleAimShooterGameModuleRuntime` as `aim_shooter`. Battle setup starts the opening module through `IGameModuleActionRunner.Start(...)`; for `turn_qte`, the injected `IBattleTurnQteModuleController` owns lifecycle, turn calculation, turn advancement, player/enemy turn begin, player action input, target confirmation, player attack/skill/item execution, enemy action, defense QTE resolution, action completion, inactive-module guards, and pending QTE cleanup.
- Do not add new QTE turn-loop, player input, target confirmation, player action execution, enemy action, defense QTE, or action-completion branches directly to battle setup. Start/re-enter QTE combat through the Game Module Runner and route QTE-specific flow through `IBattleTurnQteModuleController`.
- Player action and target-selection callbacks owned by the existing QTE menu should only execute while `turn_qte` is the active Game Module. If another module owns input, block or reroute those callbacks through that module instead of falling back to QTE attack/skill/item execution.
- Battle Game Module UI ownership should go through `IBattleGameModulePresentationController` / `BattleUIController.ApplyGameModulePresentation(...)`. A non-QTE module must explicitly disable legacy Turn QTE input, hide QTE targeting/defense surfaces, and own its presentation state before its own gameplay loop begins.
- `aim_shooter` currently has a registered presentation/input-ownership shell, an `IBattleAimShooterModuleController` lifecycle seam, and a pure `BattleAimShooterCombatSession` rule core for target validation, damage command requests, shot counts, and outcome reporting. Do not assume projectile, mouse aiming, VFX, or module-specific UI gameplay exists until those contracts are added behind that controller/module runtime.
- Battle setup stores the active `IBattleAimShooterModuleController` on `BattleManager.AimShooterModuleController` only as a lookup seam for future input/projectile adapters. Do not add mouse-aim or shot policy branches directly to `BattleManager`; call `FireAtTarget(...)` and keep policy in the module/controller/core.
- Concrete `IGameModuleRuntime` implementations receive `GameModuleRuntimeContext` in `Enter`, `Exit`, and `Start`. Use this context to read `BattleSession`, call `ParticipantCommands`, and write shared flags through `BattleFlags`; do not make each module manually reach into `BattleManager` for battle state, HP/MP mutation, or shared phase facts.
- Concrete `IGameModuleRuntime` implementations should report module-local completion through `GameModuleRuntimeContext.ModuleEvents` / `IGameModuleEventSink.PublishGameModuleCompleted(...)`. Battle Scenario Data should own reactions to module completion and `OutcomeId` through `BattleEventType.GameModuleCompleted`; do not add shooter/boxing/QTE outcome branches directly to `BattleManager`.
- Existing `SkillData.ActionTimeline` can be invoked from scenarios through `battle.skill.timeline`, `ISkillTimelineRunner`, and the concrete `BattleSkillTimelineRunner` injected by `BattleManager`. Treat this as a compatibility adapter; do not move whole-battle phase logic, post-skill cleanup, or module transition policy back into SkillData.
- Enemy-only attack authoring stays on the existing `SkillData.ActionTimeline`. `EnemyAttackAuthoringAnalyzer` is the shared source for Odin timeline preview and Project Content Validation; do not duplicate timing validation in editor windows.
- Custom `SkillActionBlock` types should override `GetAuthoringTiming()` so the authored timeline remains inspectable.
- Use `Action_Move.AttackStaging` and `PositionManager` for attacker-to-target placement. Do not serialize battle-camera or world absolute coordinates into an attack block.
- Ordinary battle uses finite DOTween framing transitions (overview ×0.70, 0.16s OutCubic, 0.18s return). No constant drift/breathing zoom. Reframe only on meaningful movement (0.75 units) or lens change; keep multi-target margins. Approved hit cuts hold preparation0.05s without changing gameplay time, then enter0.025/hold0.025/return0.075 using2.5%zoom and0.1direction offset. Coalesce active/0.12s repeats, never queue late cuts. QTE's actual defense hold suppresses cuts; after its disposal a confirmed hit may cut during grace. New defense restores pre-cut final CameraState. Pause/resume interrupted framing Tweens instead of killing and leaving the cached destination unreachable. Preserve Timeline/token/authored beat/static/shake0 priority and HUD independence.
- Skill camera beats must keep the owning battle command token. CameraController owns roll/zoom/movement; only an authored player skill may request 180→360 roll. Normalize full turns before restoring and ignore stale token cleanup. Screen shake off suppresses automatic impact zoom and skill roll.
- Battle HUD/QTE uses `BattleHudViewport`: camera-independent overlay, fixed 640x480 child root fitted to the output pixel viewport, no serialized hierarchy migration. Keep world-tracked popups separate. Re-registration must not switch this Canvas back to ScreenSpaceCamera. System modal/pause temporarily hides only the HUD Canvas; result/save/fade overlays retain priority.
- `BattleCameraActionScope` starts the dynamic shot for opposing combatants, with existing group framing for same-side actions. Strict pixel quantization is always retained; do not reintroduce the former temporary bypass or native-extension toggles. Automatic skill/hit zoom and camera roll are ignored in strict battle mode. CameraController owns Lens/Follow; SkillData never writes them. `BattleDefenderPresentationScope` owns pair staging/defense anchor/home restoration. Default approach/OriginalPos blocks stay staged until root cleanup; explicit special moves remain authored. X remains world-left1.5 units. AoE stages one representative without changing damage targets.
- C success presentation belongs to `BattleLinkCounterService`: defender parry + left recoil → immediate lunge → ready/attack → paired home return. PositionManager exposes recoil distance/duration and lunge duration. Use `PlayCounterParry`, not ordinary parry's anchor-restoring color cleanup. Damage/AP/turn ownership is unchanged. Release the counter's camera hold before retargeting the same shot; never start an unowned replacement camera command.
- Every coroutine that starts battle framing must release its own scope in `finally`. Module exit, run, battle end, seamless cleanup, and BattleManager destruction must also cancel the active Turn QTE camera presentation.
- Scope cleanup may reset only when its `CameraCommandToken` is still current. Never let an older action overwrite a newer Timeline, focus, or framing command.
- Timeline camera ownership remains exclusive through `CameraControlLease`. Additive impulse may continue during Timeline control, but ordinary tracking, reset, and Lens tween commands must not bypass the lease.

## Defense / QTE Intent

- Default defense uses Z persistent guard / fresh Just Guard, X dodge and C Link Counter. Guard multiplier0.35. Success windows are authored/default Z0.12/X0.22/C0.18s, with battle late grace0.03s; never reintroduce0.24/0.40 success minima. WithBattleAssistance preserves visible CueWindow separately (including existing melee warning duration), not a broader precision window. Clamp to actual attack duration. First cue-period attempt and actor-owned shared rearm apply to Z/X/C; automatic attack QTE never changes defense.
- Enemy attacks use a passive realtime defense window only. Never call the presentation-bearing `StartDefenseQTEWithResult` from an enemy turn and never show the defense QTE panel/event stream for enemy attacks; use `StartBattleDefenseWindow`.
- Z/X/C are read by `PlayerController` during `BattleState.EnemyAction` and enemy-skill `BattleState.ActionExecute` even while movement/GameState input is locked. When `QTEManager.IsSkillQteActive` is true, the player-skill sequence owns those keys and defense buffering pauses. The input window is opened by the active attack and closes independently from the reaction animation, so a Just Guard/evade visual is not reset to Idle before it is seen.
- Realtime battle commits only the first valid press inside that key's own success window. Early, wrong-key and ambiguous presses do not lock a later valid attempt. Accepted input cannot refresh or be reused for another impact. Preheld Z remains ordinary guard. Use InputAction event timestamps, not the frame which processed them; battle-only late grace is 0.06s. Without an accepted press, hold the attacker/projectile at contact until a valid press or timeout; otherwise resolve at original impact. Never roll damage back.
- Melee normal attacks start visible cue, START animation, ping and allowed Z together. Do not reintroduce a dim, un-parryable preparation interval. Projectile/special attacks retain wider X preparation then precise ping (normal Z, dodge-only X, Counterable min(X,C)). All derive from the assisted request. Accepted melee Z gets one local character-color acknowledgement; actual parry animation/VFX/AP/damage resolution stays at impact, never at early key press. `QTEManager` owns cue lifetime; warm-up/OnEnable never plays audio.
- `BattleImpactTiming` maps realtime to authored attack time: local 0.10s at 0.5 rate, normally adding 0.05s to the deadline. Only the owning enemy Animator and synchronized projectile are slowed; no global Time.timeScale. `BattleAttackMotionScope` restores owned speed/update mode on completion, cancellation and destroyed attacker. Pause input/cue/progression together.
- Timelines execute through `SkillContext.ExecuteBlock`. Adjacent enabled defense→projectile/sequence-melee pairs defer the defense into the impact consumer; projectile flight and per-target melee use the same clock. Never mutate serialized blocks for this; use the runtime copy. Scenario and normal battle must share this path. Bare legacy Damage still requires an authored defense block; do not infer clip hit frames.
- Just Guard prevents damage and grants configured base AP under the shared per-enemy-action per-ally budget. Dodge prevents damage without default AP or counter damage; explicit equipment trigger effects remain separate. Counterable6 and CounterOnly7 both accept C only and reject Z/X; C success interrupts the remaining skill and uses the existing counter choreography. Legacy JumpOnly/DodgeOnly/DodgeOrJump map to dodge-only in active defense. Preserve serialized numbers and attack-skill sequence keys.
- `BattleLinkCounterService` owns the attacked living defender alone: approach, Parry, ready, Attack and paired return. Other allies stay put. Hold the enemy impact pose during approach and restore the owned motion scope on cancellation. Damage is the defender's ATK × the defense block's multiplier through normal physical damage. It does not run a player turn, consume/grant AP, call NotifyActionExecuted, tick bleed, or include reserves. Own and dispose only its Tween handles; check host/module/participant life after waits and callbacks.
- Counterable skills need a readable telegraph and enough reaction time. The Skill Maker counter template and shared `EnemyAttackAuthoringAnalyzer` guide authoring; do not rewrite existing skills automatically. Counter failure takes the authored original attack only.
- Defense visual reactions are currently tied to `PlayerController`; use adapters before moving this responsibility.
- `DefensePresentationGate` separates preview ownership from input buffering. Only the selected defender opens it. Busy dodge/hurt or a committed attempt cannot restart from repeated presses. Close on QTE result/cancel/disable without resetting the confirmed pose. An accepted different input may take over an uncommitted preview once, never a hurt reaction.
- `DefenseJudgementPolicy` owns input matching, timing grade, outcome, `PreventsDamage`, `IsCounterSuccess`, and `DamageMultiplier`; do not duplicate those switches in consumers. `ActiveDefenseAttempt` owns first-input commitment; `TimedGuardAttempt` is retained for the old expiring-guard mode.
- `QTEManager` owns one active QTE, realtime execution, cancellation, structured result events, and UI delivery.
- Pre-impact cues use QTEManager's defense clock and pooled lifetime. `BattleTelegraphCue` resolves the START AnimationClip from the prefab controller once and uses `AnimationClip.SampleAnimation` via `SynchronizeToImpact`, not repeated Animator.Play/Update(0) or AnimatorStateInfo.length. Disable automatic Animator evaluation only while the cue is leased; restore enabled state on pool return. Never set speed zero in Awake, which also freezes standalone prefab playback. Preparation holds the dim first frame; Emphasize starts animation/audio once. Default and lab pivots are Center with zero world offset; current Telegraph prefab scale is 3. Preserve authored scale/orientation, tint Counterable cyan, use actor sorting order - 1, and never stack the legacy scale/flash tween onto authored animation. Hide the renderer once authored/legacy playback finishes; never hold the last visible frame until impact. Keep the lease until QTEManager releases its reference on completion/cancel, then return to the pool exactly once. Do not shorten input windows or run an independent visual timer. Reset completion/sprite/color/scale on pool return and restart at zero on reuse. Legacy no-Animator fallback settings remain serialized but hidden. Do not play audio during pool warm-up. Display timing comes from judgement policy, not ImpactCueLeadTime. Visuals must not own damage or mutate input policy; existing default/override prefab selection is preserved.
- Normal X-dodge completion restores Idle immediately, but cancellation does not. Use `PlayerCharacter.BattleAnimationVersion` to avoid overwriting a newer Hurt/Attack/Parry command with a stale dodge callback.
- Pass the intended actor through `IDefenseInputSource`; do not assume the first party member is always the defender.
- Gameplay consumers use `PreventsDamage` / `IsGuard` / `IsPerfectParry` / `DamageMultiplier` and retain existing damage, AP, camera, and VFX owners. Basic AoE uses one representative defense window, shares damage treatment across alive front-party members, and grants AP once. A missing/cancelled QTE must not cause unavoidable fallback damage.
- Enemy impact timing is defense-clock-owned: after the ready pose, `Action_DefenseWindow.AttackAnimationLeadTime` schedules the authored attack clip before the impact deadline (finite, 0 through TimeWindow; 0 preserves legacy immediate animation). The resolved result marks the clip's hit frame, not its start. VFX and `TakeDamage` follow that result. Keep `Action_DefenseWindow` immediately before its damage consumer; legacy `AttackAnimDelay` is compatibility metadata and must not reintroduce a post-result damage wait. `DelayAfter` is consumed only after damage/status and the pending defense reaction, never before the impact.
- Every enemy attack presents one `PlayAttackReady()` pose before the attack. `EnemyCharacter` falls back through `BattleReady`, legacy `CrossCutReady`, `Telegraph`, and finally `BattleIdle`; it does not use the actual `Skill` attack trigger as a ready-pose fallback. Old Animator controllers therefore do not require an asset migration. Legacy `Action_PlayAnim`, `Action_Projectile`, and `Action_SequentialMelee` paths also add the pose when no prior impact has started.
- `Action_QTE` is player-skill-only. It starts the sequence QTE in parallel with movement/animation; only damage/status/impact consumers wait for its result. An enemy timeline containing the block must skip it without opening a player QTE UI. Defense reaction completion is consumed before the timeline/turn cleanup resets all player battle poses.
- `Action_RapidStrikes` and `Action_AerialCrossSlash` are PlayerOnly local skill blocks, not scenario Action Sequences. `QTEManager.StartSkillInputStream` owns pausable input/presentation/elapsed time and reuses QteExecution cancellation without defense events; it never applies damage. Rapid hits use a separate 0.1s schedule (50 in 5s), while prompts cycle Z/X/C every 0.65s with 0.45s windows. Missing input never stalls attacks. PlayerCharacter owns temporary rapid attack animation speed; avoid per-hit Ready/Attack trigger accumulation. Aerial spin uses a token-owned CameraController 360-degree roll, never actor Transform rotation, followed by one X prompt and three crossing strikes. Normalize full turns before upright follow-up shots to avoid reverse winding. HUD/QTE remain upright; RapidStrikes has no camera roll. Use existing TakeDamage/InvokeDamageEvent and outer staging/return/turn ownership. Scope movement/rotation/flip/animation speed, camera token and QTE cleanup; do not start independent damage coroutines.
- `SkillContext.CanContinueExecution` protects delayed damage blocks. Controller contexts carry module activity; `BattleSkillTimelineRunner` carries the action handle lifecycle. Cancellation stops following blocks and pending damage; it is not a failed guard result.

## Work Rules

- 명중 피드백은 CharacterBase의 실제 피해 경로와 CharacterHitFeedback이 소유한다. 후속 승인으로 기본 캐릭터 Animator 정지는0이며 즉시 Hurt/백색/Hit VFX·수평 반동, CameraController의 유한한 준비–명중 컷을 사용한다. PublishDamageFeedback에 중복 정지를 연결하거나 CameraController.StopFrame으로 전역 시간을 멈추지 않는다. 공격자·전조/QTE 시계·HUD/음악은 유지한다. 상태 피해는 새 정지/카메라 컷을 만들거나 진행 중 직접 피격을 취소하지 않는다. 수명·조정 규칙은 characters.clinerules와 2026-10-23-battle-impact-cuts.md를 따른다.
- HP 실제 숫자/바는 이벤트 시점에 즉시 갱신하며 BattleHealthTrailView의 재사용 잔여Image만0.12대기+0.28감소한다. 연타는 목표만 바꾸고 종료를 미루지 않는다. source 교체에는 소유 잔여Image를 재생성하고 회복/rebind/disable/destroy는 자기Tween만 정리한다. 상태 표시 두 view는 공통 BattleStatusTransitionTracker로 신규/해제/빙결 전환과 수치틱을 구분하며 최초bind/동일refresh/중첩량 변경마다pulse하지 않는다. 실제 대상/행동자 표시는 기존 청록/노랑 계약을 유지한다.
- New five-value buildup is actor-owned ElementalStatusState, not legacy N-turn StatusEffect. Skill blocks must route confirmed direct damage through SkillContext.ApplySkillHit so defense, per-target once/per-hit payload and Electric coefficient remain consistent. Do not apply status payload independently after a blocked hit. DOT/consumption uses ApplyStatusDamage: no recursion, DEF/guard/invincibility/shatter. Generic period buffs remain unchanged.
- Frozen consumes Chill at threshold, disables turn and realtime Z/X/C, then releases on one skipped own turn or next direct-hit shatter. DOT never releases it. Keep CC recovery until a real action completes; items release recovery but do not trigger new Bleed. Call ClearBattleStatusEffects only at existing battle cleanup, not reserve disable. Never tick live buildup in turn forecasts.
- Elemental damage uses the existing colored popup pool. Turn QTE service publishes standalone status HP/death rules; direct-hit Electric is covered by the original direct-hit notification to avoid duplicate threshold events. Buildup-only events do not change BattleStatsRevision and must not rebuild speed forecasts.
- BunnySlime Lab now uses one default-white Wizzel (`lab.wizzel.front`) and two entries, Bunny/ZEV. No companion previews or reserve-wave entry. Lab-only copies of Wizzel10/ZEV5 skills live in Ally/BattleLab/Showcase and Enemy/BattleLab/ZEV; original DBs/skills/Scene/Prefab and the production3+3 system remain unchanged. Retired elemental/reserve assets stay intact but are not the active loadout. Keep session preparation, teardown ownership, repair menus, builders and Source/runtime party lists consistent with one actor. Never auto-migrate on domain reload.
- `Action_PresentationAccent` is local, not a Scenario Action ID. CutIn0 is retired/no-op (no sound/wait) and must not be authored. Silhouette mirrors the real actor SpriteRenderer at local identity, current frame/PPU/flip/visibility/sorting; never auto-enlarge a portrait or darken the whole backdrop. Flash arms one next successful direct hit through SkillContext; it is nonblocking and consumed once even for AoE/50-hit skills. Place the reservation before damage, or before enemy DefenseWindow, never between DefenseWindow and its consumer. Normal completion allows its short tail, cancellation clears it. Preserve strict PP/HUD/accessibility and no extra camera/RT.
- `Action_ScreenPassSlash` is PlayerOnly and owns HUD-safe viewport geometry, camera framing scope, move-only trails, crossing-progress hit once per pass, and its own home/flip restoration. Do not duplicate its damage with another block. Existing actor/skill identities and production skill data are not used as runtime mode switches.
- Enemy ScreenWide belongs to `Action_DefenseWindow`/`BattleEnemyMeleePresentationScope`, not the player block. Prepare at a safe viewport edge, then use the copied defense block's actual TimeWindow/BattleImpactTiming. Existing Damage/SequentialMelee consumes one impact. Guard/parry stop at contact; dodge/failure continue to the opposite edge; C stays central. Scope owns trail/flip/frame cleanup and falls back to Local if framing/geometry is unavailable. Do not independently advance enemy damage on a movement Tween callback.
- Lab ZEV HP1800/phase50% is source-authored and once per session; no30% extra beat. The final phase2 flag activates the optional `BattlePhaseOverlayProfile` through flag events, not enemy-name/HP checks in the presenter. Keep Source/Runtime/Sequence hashes aligned. Core flags notify only actual changes; presentation-only observers never save progress. Four corner ribbons run behind HUD/QTE with their own SFX route/fade and cannot duck BGM or mutate shared sources/materials.
- Explicit non-default actor focus may constrain the viewport to that actor only while no ordinary battle shot or Timeline owns it. Reset/default/inactive targets restore all-participant framing. Never loosen PP/fixed Lens to make a cinematic focus work.
- Repeated Z uses actor-owned ParryRearmState, default0.18s capped at half the attack duration. Observe physical presses even before the cue; same timestamp is idempotent. Repeated blocked presses extend rearm but do not invalidate an already accepted attempt. First visible-cue input remains generous; held guard65% reduction and X/C are unchanged. Never reset history on each impact window; reset at non-enemy/modal/pause/battle/owner boundaries. The actor-side gray-to-yellow meter is reused and hidden when idle. Do not add random failures or apply this restriction to player skill QTE.
- `Action_ScreenSplit` only changes the game-camera image, not damage/actor transforms/Overlay HUD. Lab Renderer2D alone owns its RenderGraph feature; enqueue its Color input only while active (2D ignores requiresIntermediateTexture). Never add a permanent copy/extra camera/depth prepass for this sample. Tokens are per camera and stale cleanup cannot release replacements. `CanRunWithoutLivingTargets` defaults false and is an explicit opt-in for target-free presentation immediately after a killing hit; do not allow damage or target animation blocks to continue on dead targets. Cancellation and dead-caster guards remain mandatory. Turn-QTE skill callbacks must also match their current action scope so Abort/Exit can invalidate stopped-coroutine presentation.

- BattleNarrationUI owns queue-driven activation and teardown. Reject blank messages before activating it; callers must not pre-activate the panel. Disable clears stopped coroutine handles/queue/confirm state and hides the CanvasGroup. Narration consumes its accepted confirmation frame. BattleMenuUI button callbacks must share Update's consumed-input/newly-enabled-frame guards before audio or command dispatch; do not mask duplicate input with a global sound cooldown.
- 중앙 상단 내레이션은 타이핑 중 확인1회로 전체 표시, 전체 표시 뒤 다음 확인으로 즉시 종료한다. 글자 간격/유지 시간에도 매 프레임 입력을 읽고 자동 종료 메시지의 유지 시간은 한 번만 적용한다. 확인 필수 메시지에 확인 전·후 고정 대기를 추가하지 않는다. 시작 프레임과 이미 소비한 입력은 다음 메시지·메뉴·방어로 재사용하지 않으며 다른 모달의 입력을 가로채지 않는다. 짧은 BattleSpeechBubble와 파란 주 대화의 정책은 별개다.
- BattleMenuUI.Confirm locks new input before dispatch. Its already-accepted skill/item callbacks must not recheck CommandsEnabled or the consumed input frame; retain dialogue/motion/module/modal ownership guards instead. Do not move the lock after dispatch: synchronous no-target/unusable-action recovery can legitimately reopen the menu within that call.
- Fixed battle HUD: use the shared SeamlessBattleHost prefab, six queue slots and up to three stable party rows. WIZEL-first uses a serialized CharacterData reference (legacy ID fallback) and is a display-only projection, never a roster/target-index reorder. Highlight displayed queue slot zero by border only. Acting yellow background and target cyan border are independent.
- Keep command/list/description/portrait areas visible during enemy actions but disable their input. ATTACK/RUN single-row previews must not introduce extra confirmation. Battle keyboard cancel is X; consume menu transition presses before target/action callbacks so Z/X cannot leak into defense/dodge. Left/right switches command tabs, up/down selects entries directly; never require a separate Z to enter SKILL/ITEM. Retain the selected row when target selection is cancelled.
- CharacterData.BattleLargePortrait and BattleStatusIconStrip are presentation-only. Preserve status calculations, AP ownership and existing target selection. Pool list rows/turn icons; do not reconstruct them on every turn. See AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-09-26-battle-hud.md for asset/layout contracts.

- Battle UI tween ownership: PartySlotUI releases its portrait/vital/punch handles on Hide/Init and owner teardown. BattleMenuUI releases color/punch/slide/delayed callbacks on Hide/Disable/Destroy without completing them. Store handles with recycling disabled, capture the actual graphic in callbacks, and guard Unity destroyed references. SetLink only covers GameObject lifetime; it does not protect a separately destroyed Image component. Never fix UI teardown with KillAll or DOKill(true), which can complete pending actions or kill another owner's animation.

- Damage popups aggregate by runtime target + damage batch + normalized display type, never by color or shared CharacterData. Physical is white; Fire/Burn, Ice/Chill, Electric, Corrosion/Poison, Bleed use status colors. Numbers only, shared Host font30, screen-right vertical columns. BattleDamagePopupPresenter.Totals reserves pending-text width and configured maximum pulse scale (default1.18); QTE placement remains in BattleOverlayLayout. Direct/status reactions each contribute once; do not change HP/events to reduce clutter.
- Popup juice is view-owned DOTween: grow0.04s to1.12/critical1.18, commit latest total at peak, return0.12s with a brief20% highlight. First number appears immediately. Inputs during growth update pending total without restarting the deadline; inputs during return restart from bounded current scale. Keep lifetime fade separate from the pulse, cancel both on pool/owner teardown, and do not delay actual damage.
- BattleDamageBatchTracker belongs to the host. Action/skill/counter/status boundaries own scopes; normal finish holds totals0.7s then fades. Explicit SkillContext.DamageBatchId plus synchronous Activate avoids assigning parallel skill reactions to another batch. Dispose/clear is idempotent and stale scopes must not close newer actions. Unscoped external damage groups only the same frame. Pool views; release old completed same-target batches on the next action and never let MISS evict active totals. Scene/prefab changes are unnecessary. See AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-10-02-damage-totals.md.
- All skill QTE entry paths go through BattleUIController.ShowSkillQTE: prefer upper side positions, exclude projected participant sprite bounds plus prompt/result margin, preserve warmup and keep a live prompt stationary. Do not bypass this placement for authored node coordinates or alter defense timing/input. Compare overlay coordinates against the root Canvas camera, not a serialized nested WorldSpace Canvas mode.
- Ally skill QTE feedback keeps the current key/ring: white flash/fade0.10s on success; red4px pixel-snapped shake/fade0.16s on failure or wrong input. Do not show HIT/MISS/FAIL text. New prompts/reset/disable cancel old tweens and invalidate completion tokens. Normal stream completion allows the last feedback to finish; cancellation hides immediately. Legacy enemy defense result UI is separate.
- Accent silhouette Unbind/Hide/OnDisable must not reparent while an actor is changing activation state. Leave the disabled view under the actor until the next normal Bind; destroy the owned view when its presentation owner is destroyed. Preserve source pose/sprite scale rather than independently approximating the transform.

- Target presentation: `BattleDefenderPresentationScope` owns the 1-unit/0.20s horizontal defender step and temporary PlayerController anchor. Open defense only after staging; dodge/hurt must return to that temporary anchor. Return normally, restore synchronously on cancellation/module exit/disable. Do not set the permanent party slot to the staged position. `SkillContext.ReturnDefender` / `CancelPendingDefenseReaction` are required at normal/cancel root cleanup; sequential melee stages each actual target.
- Counterable ultimate samples/templates move the enemy to `MoveDest.Center` (center X, existing ground Y/Z) before telegraph. Pass the defender's home position to paired C recovery; other allies do not move. Player basic attack return is DOMove, not DOJump. Optional `Action_Move.EnemyHopHeight` affects enemies only and defaults to zero; keep authored hit-clock values unchanged.

- The BunnySlime development lab is an explicit Edit Mode menu generator, never an automatic scene/asset migration. Preserve the passive original. Its sample AI overrides `EnemyCharacter.SelectSkill`; default enemies retain random selection. `IEncounterDefeatPolicy` is opt-in for seamless training encounters; ordinary defeat still uses GameOver.
- Lab data/scene/prefabs are under `Content/Maps/Development/BunnySlimeBattleLab`; canonical scenario YAML stays under `Content/Scenarios/Source/Battle/BunnySlimeBattleLab` to preserve the existing Source path boundary. See `docs/bunny-slime-battle-lab.md`.
- Lab lighting is scene-scoped: PC/Mobile Renderer List slot 1 is the dedicated Renderer2D; default slot 0 and the shared camera prefab must remain unchanged. Use the existing scene camera override, Lab Lighting - Neon prefab and its dedicated Volume profile, not a global runtime pipeline switch. Do not replace shared character materials: Mat_PixelTreadmill already uses Sprite-Lit. Keep Pixel Perfect and defense cue timing unchanged.

- Be extremely careful when editing `BattleManager`; it has wide behavior coverage.
- If adding a new enemy action, skill behavior, or defense rule, inspect both `BattleManager` and `SkillActionBlocks.cs` for duplicate policy.
- If adding or changing battle scenario actions, Battle Event Rules, module switches, HP threshold beats, or encounter-specific flow, use `.agents/skills/hubtohome-scenario-authoring/SKILL.md` and update it when the grammar or workflow changes.
- Do not match scenario rules directly against localized enemy display names inside `BattleManager`.
- UI changes should go through `BattleUIController` or a future UI adapter rather than direct ad-hoc object search.
- Movement should use `PositionManager` where battle positions are involved.
- Before saving an enemy attack, resolve every Error in the SkillData Inspector; warnings document optional or readability-sensitive choices.
- Preserve existing serialized enum numbers. Append new values unless an explicit asset migration is included.
- Do not rename animation triggers without checking `PlayerCharacter`, `EnemyCharacter`, animators, and existing assets.
- Record battle behavior changes in `AIAssets/YYYY-MM-DD-update.md`.
- Ghost trails are movement-only. Enable only around approach/dash/dodge/return movement and disable before stationary ready/attack poses. CharacterGhostTrail checks cached CharacterBase root displacement after normal DOTween movement; visual-child animation and camera motion do not qualify. Stopping/ending/disabling clears existing ghosts into the bounded pool without completing their Tweens. Do not re-enable trails for an entire attack animation.

## Authored Battle Lab / Enemy Windup (2026-09-27)

- Lab entries live in `BunnySlimeBattleLabData.Encounters`. Keep stable entry IDs, EnemyData/Scenario references, and menu text in data. Do not reconstruct runtime enemies/skills based on the selected menu index.
- Preserve the separate weak-front-line 3+3 exercise. Both main lab battles use the existing party/encounter/defense/result paths; no extra Game Module is introduced.
- `Action_EnemyWindup` is an EnemyOnly skill block, not Scenario Source grammar. Use CameraController with the current BattleCameraActionScope token; perform ready once via SkillContext, release the authored camera beat and await zoom-out before the defense window.
- The block's finally cleanup must not reset a newer shot/Timeline. Existing scene/module/action cleanup remains responsible for ending the outer camera scope.
- Full details: `docs/bunny-slime-battle-lab.md` and `AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-09-27-battle-lab-consolidation.md`.

- All SkillData, including temporary lab samples, belong under `Content/Skills`. Lab ally skills use `Ally/BattleLab`; bunny enemy skills use `Enemy/BunnySlime`. Keep skill IDs and asset/meta GUIDs when moving them. Builders must not recreate the former map-local `Data/Skills` folder.
- ZEV uses five canonical `Enemy/ZEV` Chill/Electric skills shared with the main game. Fast strike, individual-frontline sweep, delayed crash, double strike, X/C ultimate; others Z/X. Each impact uses a Center cue/defense window; no legacy UseTelegraph, projectiles or player QTE. The sweep's adjacent defense→SequentialMelee pair runs a runtime defense copy per target. See `AIAssets/yjlim/feedback/2026-10-02-zev-cold-electric.md`.
- `Action_SequentialMelee.ApproachEachTarget` defaults false. Enemy-only opt-in skips automatic central staging during preparation and steps each defender at its own position before the attacker dashes there. Defender scope owns temporary input anchor/home; block owns enemy movement and cancelled start/Idle restoration; normal enemy return remains in the root. Never reuse one defense result for every target. `RecoveryDelay` is authorable/validated, default .2s.

## Migration Sequence

Preferred sequence:

1. Wrap existing behavior with adapters.
2. Add new `ActionDirector` / `ActionSequence` support outside `BattleManager`.
3. Run legacy QTE/skill behavior through `battle.skill.timeline` and concrete `ISkillTimelineRunner` adapters.
4. Register concrete `IGameModuleRuntime` implementations through `GameModuleRegistry`.
5. Only then split `BattleManager` internals.
